    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so when sociologists talk about the \"quality\" of a neighborhood improving, how does that change play out in how the community is perceived? like, does the vibe of the neighborhood actually get \"better\" or \"safer\" as these new groups move in, compared to how it was before?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Perceptions of neighborhood quality often skew positive, as increased investment and new amenities lead to better physical upkeep and more visible commercial activity. While crime rates *can* decrease in some studies, the perceived \"safety\" often shifts from being community-focused to being tied to consumer desirability. However, this perceived \"better\" vibe often comes at the cost of cultural erasure and diminished social cohesion for long-term residents. Therefore, \"improved quality\" is a highly contested sociological concept in this context.",
      "source": "target",
